# never publish anything internal: the build spec, the milestone log, the board,
# the goal doc, or any scratch note we write to think out loud. None of it goes
# to the remote without being asked for by name.
plan.md
log.md
version.md
docs/BUILD_PLAN.md
docs/GOAL.md
context.md
NOTES.md
SCRATCH.md
TODO.md
*.notes.md
*.plan.md
.claude/

# .firecrawl/ used to be ignored, on the theory that raw pages are not graph
# data. true, and beside the point: ignoring it meant every clone had the
# citations and none of the pages, so the first thing a second machine did was
# refetch six hundred public pages we already had. the cache is committed now.
# it is 1.3M of public government html and it is the difference between "we
# fetched this once" and "we fetch this once per checkout".

# .ingest/ follows the same rule as .firecrawl/ and for the same reason: a fetch
# nobody can reuse is a fetch we will pay for again. So pages/, maps/, extract/
# and identify/ are all committed, because each one cost either a credit, a
# model call or a request to a government server.
#
# urls.jsonl is the exception. It is 12M and it holds nothing that maps/ does not
# already hold: the scoring pass rebuilds it from the maps, offline, for free, in
# seconds. Committing a derived file that large only to have it churn on every
# rescore is paying storage for a `node` invocation.
.ingest/urls.jsonl

# The raw PDF bytes are the other exception, and for the opposite reason to
# urls.jsonl: they are not derived, they are just enormous. 925 government PDFs
# is over half a gigabyte, and nothing downstream reads them. Every quote is
# checked against .ingest/pages/*.md, the page preserving text in
# .ingest/pdftext/ is committed, and .ingest/pdfs.jsonl holds a sha256 of every
# file, so the bytes are pinned without being carried. Refetching one is a
# single GET nobody will ever need to make.
.ingest/pdf/

